rous uptake prior to treatment. This highlights operational variability in clinical pathways (pre-therapy mapping vs. post-therapy evaluation) and ties the scan’s timing to facility practice patterns. [Data: Entities (5044); Relationships (11135, 11131)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Treatment centers influence workflow decisions, especially scan timing relative to RAI therapy\",\"explanation\":\"Treatment centers are included as clinical facilities where whole-body scans may be performed before RAI therapy in some settings. This indicates that implementation is not purely protocol-driven by the modality itself but can depend on local practice or resource availability at different facilities. [Data: Entities (5061); Relationships (11135)]\\n\\nThe explicit relationship connecting whole-body RAI scan practices to treatment centers suggests that decision-makers evaluating this community (e.g., for operational planning or care standardization) should consider site-level variability as an inherent feature of the network, rather than an exception. [Data: Relationships (11135); Entities (5044, 5061)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Remnant ablation represents a lower-dose I‑131 strategy used soon after thyroidectomy for selected lower-risk cases\",\"explanation\":\"Remnant ablation is described as a lower-dose iodine-131 use shortly after thyroidectomy for some lower-risk cancers to ablate remaining thyroid tissue. This places it within the early post-operative management stage and distinguishes it from higher-dose therapeutic RAI approaches aimed at known or suspected residual/metastatic disease. [Data: Entities (5049)]\\n\\nA relationship further anchors this concept by stating that remnant ablation is a lower-dose iodine-131 approach used shortly after thyroidectomy for some lower-risk cancers. Even though “thyroidectomy” appears as a related node in the relationship, the key operational point is the sequencing (post-surgery) and dosing strategy (lower-dose), which is central to risk-stratified